---
title: Shoyu Ramen
servings: 6
prepTime: 1 hr
totalTime: 3 days
difficulty: Medium
Rating: 10/10
source: Bon Appetit
tags: Pork, Soups
---

> You can find all the Asian ingredients listed here at Asian markets,
> in the Asian foods section of some supermarkets, and online.
> Look in the refrigerated section of Asian markets for fresh noodles.
> Ask your butcher for chicken necks and backs.

= Kombu Dashi and Tare =
Two days ahead: For the dashi, combine @dried kombu{2%pieces} and @water{4%qt}(cold) in a #large bowl{}.
Cover and let sit at room temperature for ~{8-12%hours}.

For the tare, combine @soy sauce{1/2%cup}(low-sodium), @dry sake{2%Tbsp},
and @mirin{1%Tbsp} in a #small bowl{}; cover and chill.

= Pork and Stock =
One day ahead: Season @pork shoulder{1.5%lb}(boneless) with @salt and @pepper.
Roll up and tie with #kitchen twine{} at 2” intervals.
(This helps keep the meat intact while cooking and makes for round, compact slices.)

Heat @vegetable oil{2%Tbsp} in a #large dutch oven{} over medium-high heat.
Cook pork shoulder, turning, until brown all over, ~{10-12%minutes}.

Add @chicken{2%lb}(necks, backs, and/or wings), @pork spareribs{1%lb}, @scallions{2%bunches}(chopped),
@carrots{2}(peeled, coarsely chopped), @garlic bulb{1%bulb}(halved horizontally),
@ginger{1%inch}(fresh, peeled and sliced), and @bonito flakes{1/4%cup}.

Remove kombu from dashi, squeezing surface oils into the liquid; discard.
Add as much kombu dashi as will fit into the pot with the pork once the liquid is boiling (reserve remaining dashi).

Bring to a boil, reduce heat, and simmer, skimming the surface occasionally
and adding remaining dashi as liquid reduces, until pork shoulder is tender
and stock has reduced to about 2 quarts, ~{2.5-3%hours}.

Remove pork shoulder from stock and let cool.
Wrap tightly in plastic and chill until ready to use.
(Chilling pork will make meat easier to slice.)

Strain stock through a #fine-mesh sieve{} into another large pot or a large bowl or container;
discard solids (including ribs and chicken). Cover and chill.

= Ramen and Broth =
> DO AHEAD: Eggs can be cooked 1 day ahead. Keep unpeeled eggs covered in cool water. Cover and chill.

Bring a medium pot of water to a boil.
Carefully add @eggs{6}(large) one at a time and boil gently for ~{7%minutes}.
(Egg yolks should be shiny yellow and almost jammy; egg white should be just set.)
Drain eggs and transfer to a bowl of ice water to stop cooking; let cool. Peel; set aside.

Remove string and thinly slice pork; cover and set aside.

When ready to serve, bring stock to a simmer; it should be very hot.

At the same time, cook @ramen noodles{6%packs}(dry, 3 oz each, discard seasonings)
in a large pot of boiling water according to package directions until al dente;
drain (no need to salt the water, as ramen noodles contain more salt than pasta).

Just before serving, divide noodles among 6 deep bowls.
Top with sliced pork, placing it off to one side.
Add tare to hot stock and ladle over pork to warm through (stock should come up just to the level of the noodles).
Halve eggs and place next to pork.

= Recommended Garnishes =
Place a small pile of @?menma{1/2%cup}(fermented bamboo shoots) or @?bean sprouts{}(fresh, or drained) next to pork.
Place a small pile of @scallions{6}(thinly sliced) next to egg.
Tuck half a @nori sheet{3}(toasted, torn in half) between side of bowl and noodles so it's just poking out.

Serve ramen with @?chili oil{}, @?sesame oil{}, and @shichimi togarashi{}, season to taste.

= Nutritional Information (per serving) =
Calories (kcal) 860, Fat (g) 34, Saturated Fat (g) 10, Cholesterol (mg) 325,
Carbohydrates (g) 77, Dietary Fiber (g) 8, Total Sugars (g) 6, Protein (g) 57, Sodium (mg) 1270
